produce more than 26 million undergarments per year.

28
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:02,200
What you're seeing now is our sewing facility.

29
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,900
We're sewing in this facility 40,000 dozens per week,

30
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,620
which are roughly 500,000 pairs of underwear.

31
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,620
300 workers in the sewing facility

32
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:19,020
piece together everything from T-shirts,

33
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:21,960
to women's underwear, to the classic style

34
00:02:21,961 --> 00:02:28,060
been around since 1935, the jockey Y-front brief.

35
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,700
The Y-front brief is made from four pieces of cotton fabric,

36
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,480
strategically sewn together with the open fly flap

37
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,940
positioned at the front.

38
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,480
Resembling an upside-down Y, the flap

39
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:46,360
provides support and easy access when nature calls.

40
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:48,560
We're the only company that produces it,

41
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:51,280
and that is what differentiates us

42
00:02:51,281 --> 00:02:53,080
from the other companies.

43
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,220
It's our trademark.

44
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:58,520
Jocky's signature brief is composed primarily

45
00:02:58,620 --> 00:02:59,820
of cotton.

46
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,420
After the cotton is cleaned and combed at Jocky's plants

47
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:07,860
in North Carolina, it arrives in Honduras.

48
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,760
At the Parkdale plant in San Pedro Sula,

49
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:14,040
this raw material must be reduced to the thin cotton yard

50
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:17,540
from which every brief is made.

51
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:20,480
First, in a process called drafting,

52
00:03:20,481 --> 00:03:24,180
rollers compress 5,200 fibers of the combed cotton

53
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,880
into one strand a half inch thick called sliver.

54
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:33,960
The sliver then passes through another machine.

55
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:36,260
These rollers are running 30 times faster

56
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,660
than the first drafting process.

57
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:41,200
And they give the yarn a tight spiral twist,

58
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:44,340
reducing its thickness even more.

59
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:47,780
And we put this material onto a bobbin

60
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:51,080
that is going to allow us to transport the material

61
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:54,880
to the next process, which is spinning.

62
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,660
The spinning process stretches the yarn

63
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:00,960
to its final width, 2 1 hundredths of an inch thick,

64
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:02,900
about the same thickness as a human hair.

65
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,200
The yarn winds up on rolls one foot in diameter.

66
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:15,280
Each roll has enough yarn to stretch more than 20 miles.

67
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:16,740
That's just long enough to get it

68
00:04:16,741 --> 00:04:21,140
to get it to its next destination in Honduras,

69
00:04:21,141 --> 00:04:25,780
Alcatex, an independent textile factory.

70
00:04:25,781 --> 00:04:28,420
Here, machines knit the yarn into the fabric used to make

71
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:31,020
all of Jockey's underwear.

72
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:34,320
Our plant is designed to produce about 1.8 million pounds

73
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:36,220
of fabric a week.

74
00:04:36,221 --> 00:04:38,360
The knitting machines are programmed to produce hundreds

75
00:04:38,361 --> 00:04:40,860
of different styles and thread counts.

76
00:04:40,861 --> 00:04:43,000
The thread count determines the thickness and strength

77
00:04:43,001 --> 00:04:46,100
of the fabric.

78
00:04:46,101 --> 00:04:52,960
We actually have 792 needles, 34 threads going into the machine at the same time.

79
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:54,480
And it has a 14 cut.

80
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:58,480
That means that there's 14 needles per inch knitting at the same time.

81
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:04,860
In six hours, one knitting machine will churn out 400 pounds of cotton fabric.

82
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,780
After bleaching, it's now ready to become a brief.

83
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,540
At yet another site, the cutting facility.

84
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:18,940
300 workers hand cut the pattern pieces that make up

85
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:22,100
the classic breed and other jockey undergarments.

86
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,500
It's not the easiest job.

87
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,840
Actually, it requires a lot of skill.

88
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:30,240
Their training for hand cutting takes around six months,

89
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:35,360
which is a long period for them to become 100% operator.

90
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:37,560
The cutters wear chain mail gloves

91
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:40,120
to protect their hands as they wind their way through the twists

92
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:43,360
and turns of the patterns.

93
00:05:43,361 --> 00:05:48,700
The fabric they cut is 200 layers thick.

94
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:54,000
To craft more exacting designs, they use a die-cutting machine.

95
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:56,040
Every week, man and machine combine

96
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:58,200
to produce enough fabric pieces to make

97
00:05:58,300 --> 00:05:59,780
over 1 million undergarments.

98
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:07,420
The next step happens at Premier Narrow Fabrics.

99
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:10,360
Machines in this factory are creating the elastic bands

100
00:06:10,361 --> 00:06:15,160
that give jockey briefs that nice snug fit.

101
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:17,000
Elastic has been holding up our underpants

102
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:20,700
since the 1930s, when it replaced cumbersome buttons,

103
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,100
snaps, and ties.

104
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:27,520
The elastic strands, consisting of spandex-coated cotton,

105
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:30,620
are woven on a mechanized loom engineered by inventor

106
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,920
Joseph-Marie Jacquard.

107
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:38,120
Behind me is a Jacquard Mueller machine, 48 hooks.

108
00:06:38,121 --> 00:06:41,560
These two weave elastic.

109
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:45,360
You can do names or patterns of designs with this machine

110
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:47,160
that automatically puts in the designs

111
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:49,660
or name in there for you.

112
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,700
Every week, Premier Narrow Fabrics

113
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,040
provides the bands for 400,000 pairs of jockey briefs.

114
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,540
Finally, the elastic bands and cut fabric

115
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:04,680
meet at the sewing facility, where skilled hands

116
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:08,120
and high-tech machinery team up to form the finished briefs.

117
00:07:08,121 --> 00:07:12,320
and boxers. For centuries, men did without

118
00:07:12,321 --> 00:07:15,820
underwear as we know it, wearing tights that were open at the

119
00:07:15,821 --> 00:07:19,060
crotch, letting it all hang loose beneath their tunic-like

120
00:07:19,061 --> 00:07:23,160
overgarments. But during the Renaissance,

121
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:26,680
when those garments got embarrassingly shorter, men

122
00:07:26,681 --> 00:07:29,680
donned pouch-like attachments called codpieces.

123
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:37,420
Some codpieces not only concealed a man's assets, but

124
00:07:37,421 --> 00:07:42,420
accentuated them. And some even doubled as change

125
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:48,100
purses. In the 19th century, the underwear

126
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:51,760
of choice for both men and women was the one piece woolen union

127
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:55,600
suit. The first ones were dyed red.

128
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:59,940
Of course, they played havoc with the wash water because

129
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:04,280
they all ran. And it was just kind of a mess.

130
00:08:04,281 --> 00:08:08,280
By the late 1800s, all Union suits were uniformly woven

131
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,280
with dull gray wool thread.

132
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:16,920
This purely functional garment was designed to keep you warm

133
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:21,040
and protect your outer clothing from body oils and odors.

134
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:23,560
Some men wore the same Union suit all winter

135
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:26,040
without ever taking it off.

136
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,340
The drop seat in the back was an ingenious way

137
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:31,440
to stay toasty in the outhouse in the cold winter months.

138
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:38,060
When you think about life pre-1900s,

139
00:08:38,061 --> 00:08:41,380
it was really all about food and shelter and underwear,

140
00:08:41,381 --> 00:08:44,180
because half the world needed to keep warm

141
00:08:44,181 --> 00:08:46,860
at certain times of year.

142
00:08:46,861 --> 00:08:50,000
Although the woolen Union suit kept its wearers warm,

143
00:08:50,001 --> 00:08:53,040
it also made them very itchy.

144
00:08:53,041 --> 00:08:56,660
But in 1887, an enterprising textile worker named George

145
00:08:56,661 --> 00:09:01,240
Munsing came up with a machine that brought much needed relief.

146
00:09:01,241 --> 00:09:04,280
He decided that he could invent a knitting machine

147
00:09:04,281 --> 00:09:08,480
that put silk threads on the inside of the knitted

148
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,680
underwear and leave the wool on the outside where it provided

149
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:13,080
the warmth and the strength.

150
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:15,220
But the silk would be next to your body

151
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:16,920
and hopefully take the itch away.

152
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:19,920
Well, did it ever.

153
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:22,040
In the 1920s, the Union suit went

154
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:25,040
through more pivotal changes.

155
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:28,580
Designers lopped off the sleeves and shortened the pants.

156
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:32,040
They made versions in cotton for the summer months.

157
00:09:32,041 --> 00:09:35,740
Then the one piece became two separate pieces.

158
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:38,520
The pants became known as boxers because they resembled

159
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:42,020
the shorts worn by prize fighters.

160
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,520
But what was a big hit in the ring

161
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:49,120
still wasn't something people spoke of in polite society.

162
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:51,520
In fact, underwear models wore masks

163
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:54,960
to hide their identities.

164
00:09:55,060 --> 00:10:00,140
But by the 1930s, that taboo was beginning to disappear.

165
00:10:00,141 --> 00:10:03,040
In 1934, a jockey marketing executive

166
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:07,740
was inspired by a postcard he received from the French Riviera.

167
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:10,220
And he looked at the postcard.

168
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:14,880
And he saw this very abbreviated swimming suit.

169
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:19,420
He looked at it, and he said, underwear.

170
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:23,300
And in 1935, when the jockey brief made its debut at Chicago's

171
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,200
Marshall Fields Department Store,

172
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:28,660
it quickly became the star attraction.

173
00:10:28,661 --> 00:10:31,500
This marked the first time that retailers dared to display

174
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:33,700
underwear in public.

175
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:35,500
And the bold move worked.

176
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:39,100
The risque design sold out immediately.

177
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:42,580
Customers had bought out the entire stock that they had

178
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:46,520
and proceeded to remove everything that was in the windows.

179
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:51,020
So it seemed to be a brief success.

180
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:53,920
But the success was anything but great.

181
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:56,580
The scandalous undergarment caught on in America,

182
00:10:56,581 --> 00:10:59,320
And the jockey team rushed fresh supplies to retailers

183
