knits one sock every 90 seconds.

515
00:29:22,281 --> 00:29:25,440
The 200,000 square foot factory hums along

516
00:29:25,441 --> 00:29:29,520
in a virtually hands-free environment.

517
00:29:29,521 --> 00:29:32,020
Except for the inspectors, who check out the goods

518
00:29:32,021 --> 00:29:34,980
as they spit out of the knitters.

519
00:29:34,981 --> 00:29:37,220
There's always many quality checks as we go along.

520
00:29:37,221 --> 00:29:39,780
Now, this young lady, this teammate of ours,

521
00:29:39,781 --> 00:29:41,680
is taking product off a knitting machine,

522
00:29:41,681 --> 00:29:45,120
and she's looking at it for any kind of defect, cross stretch,

523
00:29:45,121 --> 00:29:48,400
a hole in the product, a misplate of the yarns,

524
00:29:48,401 --> 00:29:50,340
any possible defect that may be occurring

525
00:29:50,341 --> 00:29:52,300
in the knitting machine.

526
00:29:52,301 --> 00:29:54,160
The knitted units shooting out of the machines

527
00:29:54,161 --> 00:29:57,860
aren't socks yet, but open-ended tubes called blanks.

528
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:03,800
The blanks become socks here in the finishing room.

529
00:30:06,980 --> 00:30:09,400
If you've ever wondered why every pair of socks you own

530
00:30:09,500 --> 00:30:12,240
has that seam across the toes, this

531
00:30:12,340 --> 00:30:15,320
is the step that explains it.

532
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:17,180
There are two different methods.

533
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:18,860
One incorporates a human assist.

534
00:30:18,861 --> 00:30:24,160
And the other is completely automatic.

535
00:30:24,260 --> 00:30:27,500
It's accomplished by this Rube Goldberg-esque contraption,

536
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:30,600
invented by the company's owner, Norino Grazzi,

537
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:33,800
called the Rosso Ultra-Turn.

538
00:30:33,900 --> 00:30:36,920
And this machine basically picks up stocks

539
00:30:37,020 --> 00:30:41,040
in an automated way, actually orients it inside the machine,

540
00:30:41,140 --> 00:30:43,420
and then automatically closes the toe,

541
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:46,920
and then deposits the stock on the other side of the machine.

542
00:30:46,921 --> 00:30:50,920
So it's able to go in, identify what the sock layout is,

543
00:30:51,020 --> 00:30:55,560
flip it around, turn it, change it, get it oriented.

544
00:30:55,660 --> 00:30:58,560
And at the very end of the process, it'll close that toe.

545
00:31:02,460 --> 00:31:04,280
With the toe seams in place,

546
00:31:04,380 --> 00:31:07,280
the socks are ready for bleaching.

547
00:31:07,380 --> 00:31:10,780
This tub will hold 600 pounds of socks.

548
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:12,580
There are three pockets in the tub.

549
00:31:12,680 --> 00:31:16,520
Each pocket holds 200 pounds of socks.

550
00:31:16,521 --> 00:31:23,520
After leaching, a conveyor belt loads the socks into dryers.

551
00:31:23,521 --> 00:31:28,520
And oddly enough, not one is ever lost.

552
00:31:28,521 --> 00:31:32,520
I wish I knew what happened to our socks in the dryer at home,

553
00:31:32,521 --> 00:31:35,520
but I can't seem to come out with the same amount that I put in.

554
00:31:35,521 --> 00:31:38,520
But here, we do keep up with all of them.

555
00:31:38,521 --> 00:31:42,520
Any given pair of socks might have as many as 10 different

556
00:31:42,521 --> 00:31:44,520
fibers woven in.

557
00:31:44,521 --> 00:31:48,520
Everything from cotton to spandex, a far cry from the

558
00:31:48,620 --> 00:31:52,220
earliest socks. For hundreds of years, the men and

559
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:55,660
women who could afford them wore long foot and leg coverings made

560
00:31:55,760 --> 00:31:59,060
typically of cotton or silk, called stockings.

561
00:31:59,160 --> 00:32:03,080
But in the 19th century, as men's pants got longer, the term got

562
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:08,880
shorter, and socks became a part of our underwear vocabulary.

563
00:32:08,980 --> 00:32:11,920
This era also witnessed the rise of the first knitting mills in

564
00:32:11,921 --> 00:32:15,060
mills in America, in which machines took over the knitting

565
00:32:15,061 --> 00:32:18,420
work previously done by hand.

566
00:32:18,421 --> 00:32:21,060
This really goes back to the 1800s.

567
00:32:21,061 --> 00:32:22,560
It was a lot faster than hand knitting,

568
00:32:22,561 --> 00:32:24,360
but you could knit parasoxin.

569
00:32:24,361 --> 00:32:28,680
Basically, you could form long tubes with this.

570
00:32:28,681 --> 00:32:31,080
You could make a parasoxin two or three hours

571
00:32:31,081 --> 00:32:35,280
versus 15, 16, 17 hours doing it by hand.

572
00:32:35,281 --> 00:32:38,020
Machines like this began the march toward the high-tech

573
00:32:38,021 --> 00:32:41,920
wizardry creating today's socks.

574
00:32:41,921 --> 00:32:44,660
But making socks is one thing.

575
00:32:44,760 --> 00:32:47,520
Making sure they measure up to consumer expectations

576
00:32:47,620 --> 00:32:49,360
is another.

577
00:32:49,460 --> 00:32:52,160
That's the job of the Hosiery Technology Center,

578
00:32:52,260 --> 00:32:56,660
part of Catawba Valley Community College in North Carolina.

579
00:32:56,760 --> 00:32:58,640
It has a team of researchers and experts

580
00:32:58,740 --> 00:33:01,580
who act as the watchdogs of the sock industry.

581
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:04,380
The ABLE staff carefully analyzes the properties

582
00:33:04,480 --> 00:33:07,880
of the fibers used in socks, making sure that what the label

583
00:33:07,980 --> 00:33:11,920
says is what you get.

584
00:33:11,921 --> 00:33:15,520
One of the technology center's tests determines a sock's

585
00:33:15,620 --> 00:33:17,620
durability.

586
00:33:17,720 --> 00:33:19,920
This machine cycles a sandpaper-like material

587
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:24,160
called Trizac back and forth across the socks.

588
00:33:24,260 --> 00:33:26,960
Most are tested over the course of two days,

589
00:33:27,060 --> 00:33:31,880
but some very hardy socks will make it more than a month.

590
00:33:31,980 --> 00:33:35,180
The team also conducts research on innovations that claim to do

591
00:33:35,280 --> 00:33:38,520
everything from preventing blisters to neutralizing

592
00:33:38,620 --> 00:33:40,580
stinky feet.

593
00:33:40,581 --> 00:33:45,320
Foot odor is caused by bacteria that grows in moist areas.

594
00:33:45,420 --> 00:33:47,320
And your feet is a perfect place,

595
00:33:47,420 --> 00:33:48,820
because it's inside the shoe.

596
00:33:48,920 --> 00:33:50,120
You've got sweat.

597
00:33:50,220 --> 00:33:51,320
You've got moisture.

598
00:33:51,420 --> 00:33:53,720
It's a perfect breeding ground.

599
00:33:53,820 --> 00:33:58,640
There are 250,000 sweat glands in one human foot.

600
00:33:58,740 --> 00:34:01,200
Bacteria on the skin consume the sweat

601
00:34:01,300 --> 00:34:05,400
and excrete waste that causes the strong, foul odor.

602
00:34:05,500 --> 00:34:08,340
Scientists have found that knitting silver nanoparticles

603
00:34:08,341 --> 00:34:13,140
nanoparticles into socks help solve the problem.

604
00:34:13,141 --> 00:34:16,480
The silver has inherent antimicrobial properties

605
00:34:16,481 --> 00:34:20,080
that enable it to kill bacteria.

606
00:34:20,081 --> 00:34:22,580
And it can comprise from 2% to 80%

607
00:34:22,581 --> 00:34:26,300
of these odor-eating socks.

608
00:34:26,301 --> 00:34:28,460
While the thread's technology is new,

609
00:34:28,461 --> 00:34:31,460
the cure is a time-honored tradition.

610
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:34,100
I've been told that one of the reasons

611
00:34:34,101 --> 00:34:37,900
a lot of the early sailors used to use silver canteens

612
00:34:37,901 --> 00:34:40,540
is because they knew of its ability

613
00:34:40,541 --> 00:34:42,080
to kill a lot of the microbes.

614
00:34:42,081 --> 00:34:45,400
So if you're using 100-year-old technology now just

615
00:34:45,401 --> 00:34:49,680
to make better socks, that's essentially what we're doing.

616
00:34:49,681 --> 00:34:51,720
Making socks that are good for our feet

617
00:34:51,721 --> 00:34:54,580
can be a strain on the arms and wrists.

618
00:34:54,581 --> 00:34:56,380
So workers at the Kaiser Rock plant

619
00:34:56,381 --> 00:35:00,080
do exercises at shift changes to prevent carpal tunnel syndrome.

620
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:05,520
But then it's back to work, steaming, folding,

621
00:35:05,521 --> 00:35:07,660
and packaging the socks.

622
00:35:07,661 --> 00:35:10,160
So now we've been through the entire production process,

623
00:35:10,420 --> 00:35:12,240
and we end up with a finished product.

624
00:35:12,720 --> 00:35:14,380
And I have a couple examples to show you.

625
00:35:15,020 --> 00:35:18,900
First, under the no-nonsense name, beautiful women's socks.

626
00:35:19,460 --> 00:35:22,720
And under the men's name in Burlington, great socks,

627
00:35:22,880 --> 00:35:24,560
great quality, all made in the United States.

628
00:35:26,340 --> 00:35:29,560
Another technological breakthrough on the horizon for sock making

629
00:35:29,561 --> 00:35:31,880
promises to keep us light on our feet.

630
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:37,460
A fabric called hollow fiber harnesses the power of the sun

631
00:35:37,461 --> 00:35:43,360
to help our socks relieve pain and increase energy and strength.

632
00:35:43,361 --> 00:35:46,600
Introduced into the world of sock making in 2003,

633
00:35:46,601 --> 00:35:48,940
it contains microscopic lenses embedded

634
00:35:48,941 --> 00:35:52,680
into the actual polyester fibers.

635
00:35:52,681 --> 00:35:55,420
These lenses channel available sunlight permeating

636
00:35:55,421 --> 00:35:58,920
the exposed part of the sock to increase blood flow and oxygen

637
00:35:58,921 --> 00:36:00,120
saturation in the foot.

638
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:07,420
The holofiber uses light not only from the sun,

639
00:36:07,421 --> 00:36:10,720
but perhaps also from the foot itself.

640
00:36:10,721 --> 00:36:14,120
The theory is this, that your body gives off energy

641
00:36:14,121 --> 00:36:16,620
all the time in the form of both heat and light.

642
00:36:16,621 --> 00:36:19,200
People who wear socks that have hollow fiber

643
00:36:19,201 --> 00:36:21,300
talk about having more energy.

644
00:36:21,301 --> 00:36:22,940
They're more comfortable.

645
00:36:22,941 --> 00:36:26,540
They wear them on long flights, and their feet don't swell.

646
00:36:26,541 --> 00:36:30,280
In this test, we see one foot standing on hollow fiber

647
00:36:30,281 --> 00:36:33,720
and one foot on standard cotton fiber.

648
00:36:33,721 --> 00:36:37,280
Both feet have been exposed to cold temperatures.

649
00:36:37,281 --> 00:36:40,520
reaches normal temperature almost immediately,

650
00:36:40,620 --> 00:36:42,280
while the other one takes several minutes.

651
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:47,220
If only holofiber could help you locate your sock

652
00:36:47,320 --> 00:36:52,020
after it mysteriously fails to emerge from your dryer.

653
00:36:52,120 --> 00:36:55,040
While you're searching for it, ponder this.

654
00:36:55,140 --> 00:36:57,640
What's the secret ingredient added to the underwear

655
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:02,140
of firefighters, race car drivers, and football players?

656
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:10,580
As these race car drivers thunder around the course

657
00:37:10,680 --> 00:37:13,680
at perilous speeds, their special underwear

658
00:37:13,780 --> 00:37:17,220
just may be saving their lives.

659
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:19,220
Wondering how?

660
00:37:19,320 --> 00:37:21,720
Over the course of a race, the temperature

661
00:37:21,820 --> 00:37:23,860
inside the driver's compartment reaches as high

662
00:37:23,960 --> 00:37:27,340
as a scorching 140 degrees Fahrenheit.

663
00:37:27,440 --> 00:37:29,940
Heat stroke can occur if the body's temperature rises

664
00:37:30,040 --> 00:37:31,980
above 105 degrees.

665
00:37:32,080 --> 00:37:35,280
But the danger kicks in before that fatal threshold.

666
00:37:35,281 --> 00:37:36,480
threshold.

667
00:37:36,580 --> 00:37:38,380
When you get too hot, your blood

668
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:42,380
starts to go to your skin for cooling, for perspiration.

669
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:46,020
And when that happens, the heart is pumping to get it there.

670
00:37:46,120 --> 00:37:48,120
And then your major organs, including your brain,

671
00:37:48,220 --> 00:37:51,320
are not functioning on the blood supply they normally need.

672
00:37:51,420 --> 00:37:54,040
This can impair decision-making ability, which

673
00:37:54,140 --> 00:37:56,840
can prove deadly in a high-speed race.

674
00:37:56,940 --> 00:37:59,980
The solution is the cool shirt.

675
00:38:00,080 --> 00:38:03,180
Cold water is pumped in throughout all the tubes.

676
00:38:03,181 --> 00:38:06,680
back and the front side of me.

677
00:38:06,780 --> 00:38:10,220
I basically wear it underneath my fire suit.

678
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:12,220
It's a simple cotton t-shirt that

679
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:15,820
has 45 to 60 feet of 1 1⁄8 inch vinyl tubing sewn

680
00:38:15,920 --> 00:38:17,020
onto the front and back.

681
00:38:19,560 --> 00:38:21,700
We break the cooling of the garment

682
00:38:21,800 --> 00:38:23,640
into four cooling zones.

683
00:38:23,740 --> 00:38:27,640
And each one of the lines is within two inches of another

684
00:38:27,740 --> 00:38:31,340
line, so you don't have hot spots or cold spots.

685
00:38:31,341 --> 00:38:34,180
Two small hoses on the shirt plug into a connector

686
00:38:34,280 --> 00:38:35,780
on the driver's side of the car.

687
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:42,080
As I hop in, you'll see the tubes connected to right here.

688
00:38:42,180 --> 00:38:46,080
And basically, all I have to do is plug these in.

689
00:38:46,180 --> 00:38:48,100
This connects the shirt to a cooler

690
00:38:48,200 --> 00:38:51,000
that circulates four ounces of 45-degree water

691
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:54,600
through the entire shirt every 10 seconds.

692
00:38:54,700 --> 00:38:56,300
This is a really basic system.

693
00:38:56,400 --> 00:38:58,600
This is where the ice and the water live.

694
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:01,340
There's a pump that pumps cold water through the air.

695
00:39:01,341 --> 00:39:04,080
over to my cool shirt just plugged into the car

696
00:39:04,180 --> 00:39:05,040
and into my suit.

697
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:07,380
Pretty basic.

698
00:39:07,480 --> 00:39:09,580
Rich Schaefer, a medical supply salesman

699
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:13,080
and racing enthusiast, began manufacturing the cool shirt

700
00:39:13,180 --> 00:39:14,180
in 1998.

701
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:18,900
He was inspired by a pioneering effort

702
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:21,400
during the early days of the space program.

703
00:39:21,500 --> 00:39:25,660
It started back in the 60s with NASA, with the astronauts,

704
00:39:25,760 --> 00:39:28,400
and cooling them off when they put their suits on.

705
00:39:28,500 --> 00:39:30,160
They could not get rid of the body heat.

706
00:39:30,161 --> 00:39:32,300
of body heat.

707
00:39:32,400 --> 00:39:35,040
Ness's researchers found that when the outside temperature

708
00:39:35,140 --> 00:39:39,600
rises up to 95 degrees, a person can make up to 60 mistakes

709
00:39:39,700 --> 00:39:41,800
an hour.

710
00:39:41,900 --> 00:39:44,400
Bringing the temperature down just 5 degrees

711
00:39:44,500 --> 00:39:49,280
reduces the number of mistakes to an average of 9 per hour.

712
00:39:49,380 --> 00:39:51,520
Ness's liquid-cooled garment consisted

713
00:39:51,620 --> 00:39:56,660
of 300 feet of 3 16-inch tubing sewn onto a mesh bodysuit

714
00:39:56,760 --> 00:39:57,820
worn under the space suit.

715
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:03,960
The astronauts carried backpacks that circulated cooled water

716
00:40:04,060 --> 00:40:04,900
through the tubing.

717
00:40:07,900 --> 00:40:12,800
It was first used in 1969 on the Apollo 9 flight.

718
00:40:12,900 --> 00:40:15,500
Rich Schaefer found that in most cases on Earth,

719
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:18,220
a full-body suit wasn't necessary.

720
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:20,780
Just bringing down the core temperature at the chest,

721
00:40:20,880 --> 00:40:23,920
abdomen, and back areas alleviates the problems of heat

722
00:40:24,020 --> 00:40:25,220
stress and heat stroke.

723
00:40:25,221 --> 00:40:26,020
and heat stroke.

724
00:40:29,960 --> 00:40:31,620
In Atlanta, Georgia, the cool shirt

725
00:40:31,621 --> 00:40:34,360
has been called into active duty for the hazardous materials

726
00:40:34,361 --> 00:40:38,200
team of the Henry County Fire Department.

727
00:40:38,201 --> 00:40:41,140
Their protective suits may keep them safe from toxic chemicals

728
00:40:41,141 --> 00:40:44,500
and other dangers, but they can also send core temperatures

729
00:40:44,501 --> 00:40:47,940
skyrocketing.

730
00:40:47,941 --> 00:40:51,320
After returning from a mission, each man hooks up his cool shirt

731
00:40:51,321 --> 00:40:55,460
to accelerate his recovery procedure, called rehab.

732
00:40:55,560 --> 00:40:59,920
In the Level A suits, you do get extremely hot inside there,

733
00:41:00,020 --> 00:41:01,460
and you lose a lot of your body fluids.

734
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:03,160
And if we can keep the core temperature of the personnel

735
00:41:03,260 --> 00:41:05,260
down, the quicker we can rehab them.

736
00:41:05,360 --> 00:41:06,860
Really, I can't feel it, you know,

737
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:08,080
circulating anymore.

738
00:41:08,180 --> 00:41:09,780
But I can tell it's there, because my temp's going down.

739
00:41:15,820 --> 00:41:17,580
Many firefighters wear a different version

740
00:41:17,680 --> 00:41:20,420
of the cool shirt, called the cool vest,

741
00:41:20,421 --> 00:41:22,620
rehabilitate after battling a fire.

742
00:41:22,720 --> 00:41:25,860
Recently, a local fire academy here,

743
00:41:25,960 --> 00:41:28,460
they had a death because during training,

744
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:32,060
heat exhaustion was a problem.

745
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:34,560
Firefighters go into a burn building,

746
00:41:34,660 --> 00:41:37,780
and in that burn building, they experience temperatures

747
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:39,780
exceeding 400 degrees.

748
00:41:39,880 --> 00:41:41,180
That's even in training.

749
00:41:41,280 --> 00:41:43,380
They're wearing 70 pounds of gear.

750
00:41:43,480 --> 00:41:47,480
And so they get put under a lot of heat stress.

751
00:41:47,580 --> 00:41:48,880
For these firefighters,

752
00:41:48,881 --> 00:41:51,720
the vest is a lifesaver.

753
00:41:51,820 --> 00:41:54,820
And for many surgeons, it's the ideal way to stay cool

754
00:41:54,920 --> 00:41:59,720
as they save lives during demanding sessions in the OR.

755
00:41:59,820 --> 00:42:03,220
It's like the feeling you get when you dive into a lake

756
00:42:03,320 --> 00:42:04,620
to get refreshed.

757
00:42:04,720 --> 00:42:07,860
If you're working and you know that you have a two-hour case

758
00:42:07,960 --> 00:42:11,540
ahead of you, it makes you feel secure that it will keep you

759
00:42:11,640 --> 00:42:14,480
comfortable throughout the procedure, not only for our

760
00:42:14,580 --> 00:42:17,180
comfort, but for the patient's benefit as well.

761
00:42:17,181 --> 00:42:20,080
I would say don't go into the operating room without it.

762
00:42:23,580 --> 00:42:26,260
In years to come, cool underwear

763
00:42:26,360 --> 00:42:30,060
may also become a must on the gridiron.

764
00:42:30,160 --> 00:42:32,400
These football players at Union Grove High School

765
00:42:32,500 --> 00:42:35,360
in McDonough, Georgia, are among those leading the way.

766
00:42:37,940 --> 00:42:40,400
We're out here practicing in 100 degree weather,

767
00:42:40,500 --> 00:42:43,180
95 with the humidity so high here in Georgia.

768
00:42:43,280 --> 00:42:46,140
It's just going to be a great safeguard for these kids.

769
00:42:46,141 --> 00:42:49,440
It's actually really cold, but it's very beneficial.

770
00:42:49,540 --> 00:42:51,640
